Don't drain the beans!

Prep the fresh tomatoes, if using. Wash, and then add @tomatoes{1%lb}(fresh) to a food processor and pulse until pureed. Or grate the tomatoes using the wide holes of a box grater.

Make the sofrito. Heat #large sauté pan{} over medium heat with @extra virgin olive oil{1½%tbsp}. Once hot, add @onion{1%small}(diced) with a pinch of salt and cook until the onion has softened and is golden but not browned, ~{5-6%minutes}, stirring occasionally. Add @garlic{4%cloves}(chopped), @jalapeño{1}(diced), @ground cumin{½%tsp}, @dried Mexican oregano{1½%tsp}, and @chipotle chile flakes{½%tsp}(optional) and cook for ~{1-2%minutes}, stirring frequently.

Add the diced tomatoes. Adjust the heat as needed to maintain a rapid simmer and cook until the tomatoes break down and most of the liquid has evaporated, ~{8-10%minutes}. Season with @kosher salt{1½%tsp} and several cracks of @ground black pepper{}.

Pour @black beans{2%15-oz cans}(undrained) with their liquid into the sofrito. Bring to a simmer and simmer for ~{15%minutes} until thickened and saucy. If it gets too thick, add a splash of vegetable broth if you have it, or water.

Stir in a glug of @extra virgin olive oil{}, a squeeze or two of juice from @lime{1}, and @cilantro{1%cup}(chopped). Taste, and add @kosher salt{} or more pepper or more lime juice to taste.

